WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.640
Once described as “no different
to the vomit of a drunkard”,

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:05.640
sushi has been on quite a journey.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.160
The story of sushi goes back
to prehistoric southeast Asia.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.520
There, fish was preserved by
packing it with salt and cooked rice,

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.800
resulting in a vinegary,
fermented fish, and a gloopy rice –

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.720
the rice part was truly disgusting
and usually discarded.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.080
In Japan, where
the technique remained in use

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.400
as it died out elsewhere,

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.240
it was called nare-sushi
- nare meaning aged.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:28.480
This was the definition of sushi
for several centuries.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:29.760
But by the 15th Century,

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.040
especially in coastal areas

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.280
where fish was more
plentiful all year round,

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.520
people were leaving it for less time
- and daring to eat the rice,

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.960
which, eaten early,
was pleasantly tangy.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.360
Two hundred years later,

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.840
with Japanese demand
for the taste of sushi booming,

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:44.720
an even quicker version was invented,

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.560
which involved simply adding vinegar
to the rice to flavour it.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:50.360
Finally in the 1820s,
in what is now Tokyo,

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.600
raw fish - as fresh as possible
- was added to the rice,

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.040
now no longer aged at all,
and modern sushi was born.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.360
The Japanese called it haya-sushi
- haya meaning fast.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.800
Japan was a very closed
country until 1854,

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:06.520
so it wasn’t until the late
Victorian period that Westerners,

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:07.800
particularly Americans,

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:10.720
started spending time in Japan
and encountered sushi.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.080
It required
an incredible level of skill.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.160
Getting it wrong wasn’t just
annoying, but potentially risky

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:19.840
- one rare delicacy, pufferfish,
which today is tightly regulated,

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.400
is highly poisonous
if badly prepared.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:24.600
It wasn’t until
the 1970s that sushi,

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:27.200
both as a luxury treat and
as a cheap street food,

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.120
started to be seen outside Japan.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.840
It first emerged in California, which
had a large Japanese population,

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.160
and followed the Japanese
across the world.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.600
Californians, initially aghast
at the idea of eating raw fish,

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:39.720
grew to love it.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:41.120
But, to encourage them,

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.400
Japanese sushi chefs
came up with the Californian Roll,

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.720
which originally contained
cooked crab.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.800
Other Westernised recipes followed,

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:52.040
including the decidedly 1980s
Philadelphia Roll,

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.440
with smoked salmon and cream cheese.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:57.360
Most Western countries don’t allow
fresh raw fish to be used

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.200
because of the danger
from parasites –

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.520
fish must be frozen, which
kills the parasites, before use.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.800
As sushi spread, the deeply ingrained
Western fear of raw fish

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:07.840
meant that more and more
versions came into use

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.120
which relied on ingredients such as
cooked prawns, smoked fish or egg,

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.840
and where the often complex
side sauces were replaced

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.480
with simple soy sauce,
ginger and wasabi.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:21.040
The ultimate Westernisation
is probably the Asian-Latin fusion

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.960
which is the sushi burrito,
invented in 2011.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.200
The first sushi restaurant in the UK

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.640
opened in London in 1994,
in the City –

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:31.120
the heart of businessland.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.000
Today, sushi is booming,

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:37.440
marketed as being healthy, convenient
and still just a little bit exotic.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.880
The conveyer belt concept
used in many restaurants

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:43.200
adds a certain level of novelty,
as do the chopsticks

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:44.680
- although according
to many Japanese,

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:46.520
sushi is best eaten with the fingers.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.840
Today, there are many types of sushi.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:52.680
At its best it is often still very
expensive and very beautiful,

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.360
and its devotees can
be positively fanatical.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.360
A Japanese apprenticeship lasts at
least five years for a sushi chef,

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:01.760
and both Japan and the UK
have sushi restaurants

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.200
which have been awarded
three Michelin stars.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:08.000
Not bad for a food which started
as a way to stop fish from rotting.

